<p>
Charles Choi has just released another <a href="http://yummymelon.com/devnull/opening-texinfo-web-pages-in-emacs-info.html">interesting little utility</a>. Although it’s restricted to Safari and the Mac, it’s a nice idea that could probably be ported to other operating systems without difficulty. The idea is to be able to easily move from reading a Web page generated by Techinfo to reading the same information with the Emacs Info reader.
</p>
<p>
Reading Info pages with Emacs is straightforward and comfortable but the external utilities to read them outside of Emacs are terrible. Therefore, a lot us who started with another editor learned to go to the Web first rather than try to navigate the horrible external reader packages. But, as Choi points out, recent events have shown us that we can’t always count on the Web being there for us.
</p>
<p>
Choi’s solution to all this is the <a href="https://github.com/kickingvegas/counterpart">Counterpart</a> utility that provides a menu in Safari to open the Web page’s content in Emacs. Of course, this only works for Techinfo generated content. Your immediate reaction may be, “Who cares?” but I’ve sometimes wished for something like this so that I could have the content side by side with whatever I was working on in Emacs. That also make’s it easy to copy text out of the Info buffer when you want to add a quote to whatever you’re writing.</p>
